-- title:
--   Descended symmetric functions of the conjugates lie in ℚ[j]
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime, let $\zeta$ be a unit of the cyclotomic field $\mathrm{CyclotomicField}\,p\,\mathbb{Q}$ whose underlying element is a primitive $p$-th root of unity, and let $\mathrm{conj}\colon \mathrm{Fin}(p+1)\to \mathrm{LaurentSeries}(\mathrm{CyclotomicField}\,p\,\mathbb{Q})$ be a family of Laurent series subject to two normalisations. First, $\mathrm{conj}\,0$ is the series obtained from the modular unit $u=\mathrm{modularUnitSeries}\,p = \delta\cdot(\mathrm{qExpand}_{\mathbb{Q},p}\,\delta)^{-1}$ (with $\delta = q\cdot\mathrm{dedekindEtaUnitQ}$, the $q$-expansion of the discriminant) by pushing its rational coefficients into the cyclotomic field and then multiplying all exponents by $p$. Second, for every $b\in\mathrm{Fin}\,p$, $\mathrm{conj}(b+1)$ is obtained from $p^{12}$ times the inverse of the coefficient-embedded $u$ by the twist scaling the $k$-th coefficient by $\zeta^{bk}$. Let $c\colon\mathbb{N}\to\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,\mathbb{Q}$ be such that for every $k$ the coefficient of $X^k$ in $\prod_{i}(X-\mathrm{conj}\,i)$ equals the coefficient-embedding of $\mathrm{qExpand}_{\mathbb{Q},p}(c\,k)$. Then for each $k$, $c\,k$ lies in the $\mathbb{Q}$-subalgebra of $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,\mathbb{Q}$ generated by $\mathrm{jq}=q^{-1}\cdot\mathrm{jNumQ}$.
--
--   This is the classical statement that the elementary symmetric functions of the $p+1$ conjugates of the modular unit $\Delta(\tau)/\Delta(p\tau)$ are level-one modular functions holomorphic on the upper half-plane, hence polynomials in $j$; the existence of the descended series $c\,k$ and the integrality of their coefficients are separate statements. It is used in the construction of a monic rational relation with integral coefficients satisfied by the modular unit, via [`ModularCurve.exists_monic_rat_relation_int_coeff_modularUnit`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_monic_rat_relation_int_coeff_modularUnit).
